**2. In tests, use `Microsoft.Extensions.Time.Testing.FakeTimeProvider`:**

```csharp
// Install: dotnet add package Microsoft.Extensions.TimeProvider.Testing
var fake = new FakeTimeProvider(new DateTimeOffset(2026, 1, 1, 0, 0, 0, TimeSpan.Zero));
var calc = new InvoiceCalculator(fake);

Assert.True(calc.IsOverdue(new DateTimeOffset(2025, 12, 31, 0, 0, 0, TimeSpan.Zero)));

fake.SetUtcNow(new DateTimeOffset(2024, 1, 1, 0, 0, 0, TimeSpan.Zero));
Assert.False(calc.IsOverdue(new DateTimeOffset(2025, 12, 31, 0, 0, 0, TimeSpan.Zero)));
```

**3. Production callers don't change** — the optional parameter defaults to real time:
```csharp
var calc = new InvoiceCalculator(); // uses real clock
```

### Why this is the lightest option

| Approach | Extra dependency? | DI required? |
|---|---|---|
| **`TimeProvider`** | Testing pkg only | No |
| Custom `IClock` | You write it | Usually |
| Ambient `Func<DateTimeOffset>` | No | No, but global state |

`TimeProvider` is a built-in abstract class (.NET 8+), so your library gets zero new production dependencies. The `FakeTimeProvider` NuGet package is test-only.
